WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:05.752
- We'll probably be making a decision

00:00:05.752 --> 00:00:08.166
in January or February, we're
going to see what happens.

00:00:08.166 --> 00:00:09.711
The polls have come out, they've been

00:00:09.711 --> 00:00:12.143
unbelievable strong, they say I'd win.

00:00:12.143 --> 00:00:13.521
- [McKay Coppins] The
interesting thing about Trump

00:00:13.521 --> 00:00:16.045
is that he's been threatening
to run for President

00:00:16.045 --> 00:00:19.325
for basically 25 years, all
the way back to the 80s,

00:00:19.325 --> 00:00:22.424
when he gave a speech
in New Hampshire and saw

00:00:22.424 --> 00:00:25.048
a bunch of newspapers all
over the country write stories

00:00:25.048 --> 00:00:28.091
wondering or speculating
about whether he would run.

00:00:28.091 --> 00:00:30.575
This is the first time
he's actually filed papers

00:00:30.575 --> 00:00:32.456
and is out on the campaign trail.

00:00:32.456 --> 00:00:35.393
And I think a lot of people
are surprised by that.

00:00:35.393 --> 00:00:39.619
(suspenseful music)

00:00:40.849 --> 00:00:42.684
- There are a lot of
reasons for Donald Trump's

00:00:42.684 --> 00:00:45.239
popularity right now in
the Republican Party.

00:00:45.239 --> 00:00:48.118
One of them, I think, boils
down to the celebrity effect.

00:00:48.118 --> 00:00:50.080
If you look at the polls, he does best

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:53.284
with voters who are not as likely to vote

00:00:53.284 --> 00:00:55.083
in Republican primaries.

00:00:55.083 --> 00:00:56.721
So imagine a pollster calling you up,

00:00:56.721 --> 00:00:59.739
and reading you a list of
17 different candidates

00:00:59.739 --> 00:01:01.684
and then asking who you support:

00:01:01.684 --> 00:01:05.068
chances are, Donald Trump may be
the only person you've heard of.

00:01:05.068 --> 00:01:08.307
- I'm not using the lobbyists, I'm
not using donors, I don't care.

00:01:09.277 --> 00:01:11.763
I'm really rich, I'll
show you that next time.

00:01:11.763 --> 00:01:14.287
- Trump appeals to people's ideal of

00:01:14.287 --> 00:01:16.086
what they would do if they were rich.

00:01:16.086 --> 00:01:17.746
They would have fancy suits and

00:01:17.746 --> 00:01:21.903
big, private airplanes, and
hang out with supermodels.

00:01:21.903 --> 00:01:25.757
Trump's entire persona
is sort of geared toward

00:01:25.757 --> 00:01:30.076
the dreams and fantasies
of what wealth is like

00:01:30.076 --> 00:01:33.420
for the average blue-collar,
middle-class worker.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.101
- I guess I cause controversy,
and for no reason.

00:01:36.101 --> 00:01:38.945
- A lot of people don't like
the political-correctness rules

00:01:38.945 --> 00:01:41.802
that govern how we talk,
and what we can say

00:01:41.802 --> 00:01:44.739
and Donald Trump flies in the
face of all of those rules.

00:01:44.739 --> 00:01:46.678
All the way back to his
announcement speech,

00:01:46.678 --> 00:01:49.045
when he made a lot of really

00:01:49.045 --> 00:01:51.159
crazy comments about Mexican immigrants.

00:01:51.159 --> 00:01:54.304
Or when he attacked John
McCain's war service.

00:01:54.304 --> 00:01:58.682
Or when he got into a feud
with Megyn Kelly at Fox News

00:01:58.682 --> 00:02:02.199
and suggested that during the
debate she was menstruating.

00:02:02.199 --> 00:02:05.601
He is the billionaire who
doesn't play by your rules

00:02:05.601 --> 00:02:07.900
and isn't governed by
political-correctness.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:10.140
And that's what a lot of
people like about him.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:15.140
- Mexico is sending people
that Mexico doesn't want.

00:02:15.399 --> 00:02:18.382
And everybody knows
that's what I was saying.

00:02:18.382 --> 00:02:20.666
- One reason Trump gets away with saying

00:02:20.666 --> 00:02:22.889
such outrageous, ludicrous
things all the time

00:02:22.889 --> 00:02:25.558
is because Donald Trump has a policy

00:02:25.558 --> 00:02:28.229
where he never apologizes for anything.

00:02:28.229 --> 00:02:30.609
And by virtue of never apologizing,

00:02:30.609 --> 00:02:33.036
it makes it so that
people just assume that

00:02:33.036 --> 00:02:34.672
it just wasn't that big of a deal.

00:02:34.672 --> 00:02:37.470
- I'm a Republican, I'm a conservative,

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:38.875
I'm running, I'm in first-place

00:02:38.875 --> 00:02:41.696
by a lot, it seems,
according to the polls.

00:02:41.696 --> 00:02:43.604
- [Coppins] Can he win
the nomination? Sure!

00:02:43.604 --> 00:02:47.267
(laughs)
He could win the nomination,

00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:49.876
I think most people would
still say it's unlikely.

00:02:49.876 --> 00:02:52.684
If you think back to fall of 2011,

00:02:52.684 --> 00:02:55.598
Rick Perry was leading in
the polls at that time.

00:02:55.598 --> 00:02:57.362
Of course he didn't become the nominee.

00:02:57.362 --> 00:03:01.462
Fall of 2007, Rudy Giuliani
was leading in the polls.

00:03:01.462 --> 00:03:03.157
A lot of people that lead in the polls

00:03:03.157 --> 00:03:04.596
at this point in the campaign,

00:03:04.596 --> 00:03:06.860
don't actually end up
becoming the nominee.

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:11.860
(musical tone)

